== Headship in Marriage and the Church

=== §. 14. #emph[How a husband is his wife’s head.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:23.]

    #text(style: "italic")[For the husband is the head of the wife, as Christ is the head of the church; and He is the Savior of the body.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] place of a husband, hinted at in the last clause of the previous verse, is more plainly expressed and fully explained in this verse. His place is expressed under the metaphor of a #emph[head,] and amplified by his likeness in this to #emph[Christ.]

The particle of connection (FOR) shows that this verse is added as a reason. It may fitly be referred both to the duty itself and to the manner of performing it.

The metaphor of a #emph[head] enforces the #emph[duty.]

The amplification of it, by the likeness that is drawn to Christ, enforces the #emph[manner] of performing the #emph[duty.]

A wife must submit herself to a husband, because he is her #emph[head;] and she must do it #emph[as to the Lord,] because her husband is to her as Christ is to the church.

The metaphor of a head declares two points: the dignity and the duty of a husband.

1\. As a head is more eminent and excellent than the body, and placed above it, so is a husband to his wife.

2\. As a head, by the understanding that is in it, governs, protects, preserves and provides for the body, so the husband governs, protects, preserves and provides for his wife; at least he ought to do so, for this is his office and duty. This is noted here to show the benefit that a wife receives from her husband. So two motives are included under this metaphor.

The first is taken from the husband’s prerogative. From it note that

#emph[Subjection must be yielded to those who are over us.] For this is a main end of the difference between one party and another. #footnote[Observation.] To what end is the head set above the body, if the body is not subject to it?

The second is taken from the benefit that a wife reaps from her husband’s superiority, and it shows that

#emph[Those who will not submit themselves to their superiors are] #footnote[#emph[Observation.] See #emph[Treatise] 3, §. 73.] #emph[injurious to themselves,] as the body would be injurious to itself if it would not be subject to the head.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of the resemblance of a husband to Christ.]

To enforce the reason given above all the more, the apostle adds the likeness there is between a husband and Christ, as this note of comparison#footnote[ὡς.] (#emph[as]) shows. From this it follows that

#emph[It is as fitting for a wife to submit herself to her husband as] #footnote[Observation.] #emph[for the church to submit itself to Christ.] This amplification is added especially for Christians. Heathens may be moved to subject themselves to their governors by the likeness taken from a natural body. How much more ought Christians to be moved by the likeness taken from the mystical body of Christ?

These words, “and He is the Savior of the body,” as they declare the office of Christ and the benefit that the church reaps, so they note the end for which a husband is appointed to be the head of his wife: namely, that by his provident care he may be as a savior to her. It is noted here rather to show the benefit that a wife reaps from her husband than the duty that he owes, for the apostle declares that afterwards (v. 25, etc.). The meaning, then, is this: as Christ was given to be a head of the church, which is his body, so that he might protect it and provide all needful things for it, and so be a Savior to it, even so, #footnote[How a husband is a savior to his wife. See #emph[Treatise] 3, §. 73.] for that very end, husbands are appointed to be the head of their wives.

On this ground the apostle infers the conclusion in the next verse.

=== §. 16. #emph[Of the resemblance between] the church’s relation to Christ and a wife’s to her husband.

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:24.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Therefore, as the church is subject to Christ, so let wives be subject to their own husbands in everything.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[This] conclusion sets forth not only the duty itself, but also another #emph[reason] and another #emph[rule] to stir up and direct wives to perform their duty, and that under the pattern of the church.

The #emph[reason] may be framed thus: #emph[What the church does to Christ, a wife must do to her husband. But the church is subject to Christ. Therefore a wife must be subject to her husband.]

The proposition is grounded on the likeness there is between the church in relation to Christ and a wife in relation to her husband. For a husband is to his wife what Christ is to the church; therefore a wife must be to her husband as the church is to Christ.

The #emph[rule] notes both the #emph[manner]#footnote[ὥσπερ.] (#emph[as]) and also the #emph[extent] of a wife’s subjection#footnote[ἐν παντί.] (#emph[in everything]). #footnote[See more of the #emph[manner] and #emph[extent] of a wife’s obedience, #emph[Treatise] 3, §. 51 and 63.]

#emph[Question.] Is mortal and sinful man to be obeyed as the Lord Christ, the eternal Son of God?

#emph[Answer.] This extent is to be limited to the whole range of the things in question. So it is in other places. Where the apostle says, “All things are lawful for me,”#footnote[1 Cor 10:23.] he means all things neither commanded nor forbidden, for his speech in that place was of them. And where again he says, “Eat anything set before you#super[+],” #footnote[#emph[v.] 27.] he means any good and wholesome food, for that was what he spoke of.

Thus much of the main drift of the apostle in setting before wives the example of Christ, whom husbands resemble in dignity, and the example of the church, which wives ought to resemble in duty.

I will further consider these examples of Christ and the church more distinctly by themselves, without any relation to man and wife, and from them note such general instructions as concern all Christians.

=== §. 17. #emph[Of the relation between Christ and the church.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:23, 24.]

    #text(style: "italic")[23\. Christ is the head of the church; and He is the Savior of the body. 24. The church is subject to Christ in everything.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[See] here the mutual relation between Christ and the church.

#emph[In this, note concerning Christ:]

+ His preeminence over the church, #emph[(he is her head.)]
+ His goodness to her, #emph[(he is her Savior.)]

#emph[Note also concerning the church:]

+ Her prerogative, #emph[(she is the body of Christ.)]
+ Her duty. In setting this down, there is noted
  + What it consists in #emph[(the church is subject to Christ)]
  + How far it extends, #emph[(in everything.)]

The title #emph[Head] is given to Christ in two respects.

+ In regard to his #footnote[Col 1:18.] dignity and dominion over the church.
+ In regard to the #footnote[Eph 4:15, 16.] close union between him and the church.

This union is more fully expressed afterwards, in v. 30.

The dignity of Christ is chiefly intended here, so that #emph[Christ is the highest in authority over the church.] The titles #footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[Lord,]#footnote[1 Cor 8:6.] #emph[father, master, teacher,]#footnote[Matt 23:7, 8, 9.] #emph[Prophet,]#footnote[Deut 18:15.] #emph[Firstborn,]#footnote[Col 1:18.] and the like, being attributed to him by a kind of excellence, as properly his own, prove as much.

The causes of this are

+ The good pleasure of God his Father.
+ The dignity of his person, being God-man.
+ The merit of his sacrifice, by which he has redeemed and purchased his church for himself.
+ The omnipotence of his power, by which he is able to protect it.
+ The all-sufficiency of his Spirit, by which he is able to give every member all needful grace.

Until the Pope of Rome can show as good a reason for this #footnote[1 #emph[Use.]] title #emph[(Supreme head of the whole church),] we will account him a blasphemous usurper of it.

#emph[Objection.] He is not accounted an #emph[imperial] head as Christ is, #footnote[The distinction between an imperial and a ministerial head discussed.] but only a #emph[ministerial] head.

#emph[Answer.]

+ This distinction is without any ground or warrant of Scripture.
+ It implies a plain contradiction. For to be a ministerial head is to be a #emph[head] and a #emph[minister,] which is all one with being a #emph[head] and a #emph[member] in relation to the same thing.
+ Though by these two words #emph[(imperial, ministerial)] they may seem to advance Christ above the Pope, yet in their own interpretation of these words they make the Pope equal #footnote[#emph[Staplet. lib.] 6. #emph[cap.] 16. #emph[de princip. doct.]] to Christ, if they do not advance him above Christ. For they say that Christ is an #emph[imperial] head, to give life to the church inwardly, and the Pope a #emph[ministerial] head, to govern it outwardly. First, let it be noted how little this exposition agrees with the words expounded. Does this word #emph[(imperial)] suggest a life-giving power? Does this word #emph[(ministerial)] imply a governing power? No; is there not great incongruity in this, that Christ should be the #emph[imperial] head, and yet the Pope a head to govern? Besides, does this not tear apart two of Christ’s offices, leaving one to Christ and giving another to the Pope, and so make him equal with Christ? If the particular branches of this government that papists give to the Pope by virtue of his headship are observed, we shall find verified in him what the apostle foretold concerning Antichrist, that “he will seat himself in the temple of #footnote[2 Thess 2:4.] God, proclaiming himself to be God.” For they give him the keys of heaven and hell, to shut or open the one or the other as #footnote[In Decretal.] he pleases. They give him power to dispense with God’s laws, to coin articles of faith, to make laws to bind men’s consciences directly and immediately, to give pardon for sin, to free subjects from allegiance to their sovereigns, to canonize saints, and who knows what else. But to let these impious blasphemies pass: besides the fact that this prerogative of Christ (to be #emph[head of] #footnote[Eph 1:21, 22, 23] #emph[the church]) is incommunicable (for by it the apostle proves Christ to be advanced “far above all rule and authority, power and dominion, and every name,” etc.), Christ needs no vicar or deputy for the execution of his office in it. For as head he “fills all in all,” and by his eternal spirit he is in heaven, on earth, and in every place where any of his members are, according to his #footnote[Matt 18:20; 28:20.] promises made to his church.

This must surely give much comfort and great confidence #footnote[Use.] to all who have assurance that they are of this body. For having so mighty, so wise, so merciful a head, a head so sufficient every way, who can instruct, direct, guide, govern, protect and help them in all their needs whatever, what need they fear? When we are assaulted by Satan, or set upon in any way by any of his instruments, or are in any distress or need, let us lift up the eyes of our faith higher than we can lift the eyes of our body, and in heaven behold this our head, who is invisible; and we cannot but receive much comfort and encouragement from there.

=== §. 18. #emph[Of the benefit of Christ’s headship.]

The #emph[goodness] of Christ is set down in these words: “and He is the Savior of the body.” Almost every word has its emphasis.

1\. The joining particle (AND) shows that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[The good that Christ does for his church, he does because] #footnote[καί 1. #emph[Observation.]] #emph[he is its head.]]

O how happy a thing it is for the church that it has such a head! A head that does not tyrannize over it, nor trample it under foot; a head that does not fleece or strip the church, but procures peace and safety for it. When Naomi sought to make a match between Boaz and Ruth,#footnote[Ruth 3:1.] that he might be her head, what did she say? “Should I not seek rest for you, that it may be well with you?” It is therefore the office of a head to be a savior, to procure rest and prosperity for the body whose head it is.

Happy it would be for kingdoms, commonwealths, cities, churches, families, wives, and all that have heads, if they had such heads: heads that, because they are heads, would endeavor to be saviors.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of Christ as a sufficient] Savior.

In setting out the goodness of Christ, three things are noted.

+ The #emph[kind] of goodness, which is salvation #emph[(the Savior)]
+ The person who performs it #emph[(he himself.)]
+ The parties for whom he performs it #emph[(the body.)]

1\. The Greek word translated #footnote[σωτήρ. #emph[Sotera inscriptum vidi Syracusis. hoc quantum est? Ita magnum, vt Latino vno verbo exprimi non possit. Is est nimirum soter, qui salutem dedit. Cicer. in Ver. act] 4. \[I saw “Soter” inscribed at Syracuse. How much does this mean? So much that it cannot be expressed in one Latin word. He is, in short, a soter who has given salvation.\]] #emph[Savior] is so emphatic that other tongues can hardly find a fit word to express its emphasis. Being attributed to Christ, it implies that

#emph[Christ is a most absolute and perfect Savior;] he is every way a sufficient Savior, #footnote[Heb 7:25 σῴζειν εἰς τὸ παντελὲς δύναται. Matt 1:21.] “able to save completely,” even to the very uttermost. He saves soul and body. He saves from all manner of misery, which is suggested by that particular thing from #footnote[Doctrine.] which he saves, namely #emph[sin:] “He will save His people from their sins.” Sin is the greatest and most grievous evil; indeed, it is the cause of all misery. Those who are saved from it are saved from all evil, for there is nothing hurtful to man but what is caused by sin or poisoned by it.

Before sin seized on man, he was most happy, free from all misery; and so he will be after the contagion, guilt, punishment, dominion and remainder of sin are removed. But he who remains in the bondage of sin is in a most woeful plight. In that Christ saves from sin, he saves from the wrath of God, the curse of the law, the venom of all outward crosses, the tyranny of Satan, the sting of death, the power of the grave, the torments of hell, and much else besides.

The purity of Christ’s nature and the excellence of his person #footnote[Reason.] are what make him so sufficient a Savior. The apostle himself notes this reason: for where he says that Christ is “able to save completely,” he adds, as proof of it, that he #footnote[Heb 7:25, 26.] is “holy, innocent, undefiled, set apart from sinners, and exalted above the heavens.”

This supplies #footnote[#emph[Uses.] Consolation. Luke 2:10, 11. Luke 1:47.] us with great matter for #emph[rejoicing] and for #emph[confidence.] When the angel first brought this news, “A Savior has been born to you#super[+],” he said, “Behold, I bring you good news of great joy.” This made the Virgin Mary say, “My spirit rejoices in God my Savior,” and for this Zechariah blessed God, who “has … redeemed His people” and “raised up a horn of salvation.” #footnote[#emph[v.] 68, 69.] When the eyes of old Simeon had seen this #emph[salvation,] he desired to live no longer, but said, “Sovereign Lord, … You now dismiss Your servant #footnote[Luke 2:29, 30.] in peace.”

Those who believe in this #emph[Savior] will be of like mind. As they rejoice in him, so they will trust in him, and say with the apostle, “we are more than conquerors through Him #footnote[Rom 8:37.] who loved us,” etc.

This being so, what end does the supposed #emph[treasury of] #footnote[Refutation.] #emph[the church] serve, in which are said to be stored up #emph[indulgences, pardons, merits, works of supererogation,] and I know not what trash, to add to the satisfaction of this Savior? Either Christ is not a sufficient Savior, or these things are (to say the least) vain. But vain they are; and that is an empty, filthy, detestable treasury, which God will destroy with all who trust in it.

=== §. 20. #emph[Of Christ as the only Savior.]

This relative particle#footnote[αὐτός.] (HE) also has its emphasis. For as it points out #emph[Christ the head of the church,] so it restricts this great work to him. It may be translated thus: #emph[he himself;] that is, he in his own person, he by himself, he and none but he. So, to speak properly,

#emph[Christ is the only Savior of men.] In this respect he is #footnote[Doctrine.] called “a horn of salvation,”#footnote[Luke 1:69.] indeed, “salvation”#footnote[Luke 2:30.] itself. These titles are given to him by way of #emph[excellence, as properly his own;] and in the same respect the name #emph[Jesus]#footnote[Matt 1:21.] was given to him.

Here, by the way, note the blasphemous arrogance of those great sectarians among the papists who style themselves #emph[Jesuits,] taking that name which belongs only to this great office of Jesus Christ.

#emph[Objection.] Why is this name more blasphemous than the title #emph[Christians?]

#emph[Answer.] One of their own religion resolves that objection thus: “We are called Christians from Christ, but not Jesuits from Jesus, because we share in the thing signified by the name Christ, that is, anointing; for (as the apostle says) ‘we have all received from His fullness.’ But he has not shared with us the thing signified by the name Jesus, for it belongs to him alone to save; as the Scripture says, ‘He will save His people,’ as if he should say, he alone, and no other.” #emph[A Christo dicimur] #footnote[Jesuit, a blasphemous title.] #emph[Christiani, non autem à Iesu Iesuani, seu Iesuitae: quia rem signatam nomine] Christus, #emph[scil. vnctionem nobis communicauit. Nam, ut ait Apostolus, omnes nos de plenitudine eius accepimus: sed rem significatam nomine] Jesus #footnote[Why we may be called Christians and not Jesuits.] #emph[non communicauit: nam saluare ipsi soli conuenit: ipse enim (vt dicitur in scriptura) saluum faciet] #footnote[#emph[Christus commune dignitatis est nomen. Iesus proprium vocabulum saluatoris. Hier. in Mat.] 16. \[Christ is a common name of dignity; Jesus is the proper name of the Savior.\]] #emph[populum suum: ac si diceret, ipse solus, & non alius. Guil. Lindwood in Prouinc. siue Constitut. Angl. lib. de Consuetud.]

But to return to our matter: Saint Peter most plainly and fully proves the doctrine named above in these words spoken of Jesus Christ: “Salvation exists in no one else, for there is no other name under heaven given to men by which we must be saved.”#footnote[Acts 4:12.] None is able, #footnote[Rev 5:4, 5.] none is worthy, to do so great #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]] a work. He must do it, or it cannot be done. But #footnote[Isa 63:3.] he is so #footnote[See #emph[The Whole Armour of God,] Treatise 1, part 1, §. 5.] able, and so worthy, that he can do it by himself, and needs none to assist him.

What folly it is to trust in other saviors! Papists have legions of #footnote[#emph[Vtrum multi salutares dici possint non facile audendum est. Solus enim ipse saluator corporis est. Aug. in Ios. lib.] 6. #emph[quaest.] 23. \[Whether many may be called saviors is not lightly to be ventured; for he alone is the Savior of the body.\]] saviors to whom they flee in their need. All the angels in heaven, and all whom their popes have at any time canonized as saints (and they are many millions), are made saviors by them. “Be stunned by this, O heavens,” for they “have committed two evils: They have forsaken” Christ, “the fountain of living water, and they have dug their own cisterns—broken cisterns that cannot hold water.”#footnote[Jer 2:12, 13.]

Let us for our part flee to this Savior only, and wholly #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] rely on him, as we desire to be saved. In this way we shall honor him by preferring him before all, indeed, by rejecting all but him; and in this way we shall be sure to bring help, ease and comfort to our own souls.

=== §. 21. #emph[Of the church as the body of Christ.]

The persons who receive any benefit from this #emph[Savior] are all comprised under this metaphor, #emph[the body,] by which the same thing is meant as was meant before by the #emph[church.]

#emph[Church,] according to the derivation of the Greek word, signifies #footnote[ἐκκλησία. #emph[Ecclesia ex vocatione appellata est. Aug. in Epist. ad Rom.] \[The church is named from its calling.\]] an assembly called together. In Scripture it is given, as properly theirs, to those who are called to God.

This calling is twofold:

1\. #emph[Outward,] which is common to all who make profession #footnote[Matt 20:16.] of the gospel. In this respect it is said, “many are called, but few are chosen.”

2\. #emph[Inward,] which belongs only to the elect. None but they, and all of them in their time, shall both be called outwardly by the word to a profession of Christ, and also inwardly and effectually by the spirit to believe in Christ and obey his gospel. This is styled “the heavenly calling,”#footnote[Heb 3:1.] which #footnote[1 Cor 1:2.] belongs only to the saints. These make up that church of which Christ is properly the head; and therefore, in relation to that metaphor of a head, they are called the #emph[body,] and that in these respects:

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ They are under Christ, as a body under the head.
+ They receive spiritual life and grace from Christ, as a natural body receives sense and vigor from the head.
+ Christ governs them, as a head governs the body.
+ They are subject to Christ, as a body to the head.
]

=== §. 22. #emph[Of the extent of Christ’s goodness to all his body.]

This metaphor, by which the persons who reap the benefit of Christ’s office are set forth, notes two points.

1\. #emph[All who are once incorporated into Christ will be saved.] The body comprises all the parts and members under it: not only arms, shoulders, breast, back and the like, but also hands, fingers, feet, toes and all. Since Christ their head is their #emph[Savior,] who can doubt of their salvation?

2\. #emph[None but those who are incorporated into Christ will be saved.] For this privilege is reserved for the #emph[body.]

The former point is clearly set forth by a comparison that the apostle makes between Adam and Christ, thus: “just as the trespass of one brought condemnation for all men, #footnote[Rom 5:18.] so also the righteousness of one brought justification and life for all men.” Here two roots are noted: one is Adam, the other is Christ. Both of them have their number of branches, to all of which they convey what is in them, as the root conveys the sap that is in it into all the branches that sprout from it. The first root, Adam, conveys sin and death to all who come from him; and the other root, Christ, conveys grace and life to every one who is given to him. For he says, “Everyone the Father #footnote[John 6:37.] gives Me will come to Me, and the one who comes to Me I will never drive away.” And a little after, he gives this reason: “this is the will of Him who sent Me, that I shall lose none of those He has given Me, #footnote[#emph[v.] 39.] but raise them up at the last day.”

#emph[Objection.] Christ himself makes an exception of one, where he says, “Not one of them has been lost, except the son of perdition.” #footnote[John 17:12.]

#emph[Answer.] That phrase, #emph[son of perdition,] shows that Judas was never of this body. For can we imagine that Christ is a Savior of a son of perdition?

#emph[Objection.] Why then is he excepted?

#emph[Answer.] By reason of his office and calling he seemed to be of this body, and until he was made known, no one could judge otherwise of him. In this respect Saint Peter says, “He was #footnote[Acts 1:17.] one of our number.”

2\. #emph[Answer.] Christ speaks there in particular of the twelve apostles, and to be an apostle of Christ was in itself only an outward calling.

This is a point of wonderful comfort to those who have assurance #footnote[Use.] of their incorporation into Christ: they may rest on the benefit of this office of Christ, that he is a #emph[Savior.] We need not think of climbing up to heaven and searching God’s records to see if our names are written in the book of life. Let us only test whether we are of this body or not. #footnote[How we may know whether we are of Christ’s body or not.] For our help in this, let us know that this metaphor of #emph[a body] implies two things.

+ A mystical union with Christ.
+ A spiritual communion with the saints.
  + By virtue of that union, those who are of Christ’s body
    + Receive grace and life from him.#footnote[Eph 4:15, 16.]
    + Are guided and governed according to his will.#footnote[John 17:6.]
    + Seek to honor him in all things they do.#footnote[John 17:10.]
    + Are offended and grieved when he is dishonored by others.#footnote[Gal 3:1. Ps 119:136.]
  + By reason of their communion with the saints, being fellow members,
    + They love the brethren.#footnote[1 John 4:11.]
    + They are ready to help those who are in distress.#footnote[Matt 25:40.]
    + They will edify one another.#footnote[Eph 4:16.]
    + They keep a mutual sympathy, rejoicing and mourning with one another.#footnote[1 Cor 12:26.]

=== §. 23. #emph[Of limiting the benefit of Christ’s headship to those only who are of his body.]

That none but those who are of Christ’s body will share in the benefit of his office is clear from other like titles of restriction, such as #emph[his people]#footnote[Matt 1:21.] and #emph[his sheep,]#footnote[John 10:15.] but especially from his denying to the #emph[world] the benefit of his intercession: “I do not ask on behalf of the world,”#footnote[John 17:9.] he says. In this respect this position, #emph[(out of the church, no salvation),] is true without exception. For #emph[the body] is the true, invisible universal church. He who is not a member of this church, but is out of it, does not have Christ to be his head #footnote[#emph[Ecclesia corpus Christi est: unde manifestum est, eum qui non est in membris Christi Christianam salutem habere non posse. Aug. de Unit. Eccl. c.] 2. \[The church is the body of Christ; from which it is clear that he who is not among the members of Christ cannot have Christian salvation.\]] and Savior; from where, then, can he have salvation?

The former point is not more comforting to those who have assurance that they are members of this body than this one is terrible to those who give too much evidence that they are no members of it. All those do so who do not have the spirit of Christ ruling in them, but rather rebel against him, and who bear no love to the saints, but rather hate them and do them all the spite they can.

=== §. 24. #emph[Of the church’s subjection to Christ.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:24.]

    #text(style: "italic")[The church is subject to Christ in everything.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] duty that the church performs to Christ her head, by way of thankfulness for this great benefit, that #emph[he is her Savior,] is #emph[subjection.] Under this word is comprised all the obedience and duty of any kind that Christ requires of the church, in and by the word.

#emph[Question.] Is it possible for that part of the church which is here on earth to yield such obedience?

#emph[Answer.] It will faithfully endeavor to do what it can; and that sincere and utmost endeavor Christ graciously accepts as a perfect performance of all.

In that it is taken for granted here that “the church is subject to Christ,” I may infer, as from a general to a particular, that

#emph[Whoever is of the true universal church is subject to Christ,] and yields obedience to his word. “We will run after you,”#footnote[Song 1:3.] #footnote[Doctrine.] says the church to Christ. “My sheep listen to My voice … and they follow Me,”#footnote[John 10:27.] says Christ of that flock, which is his church.

For Christ conveys his own spirit into his mystical body the church, and into every member of it; and this spirit is #footnote[Reason.] much more operative and full of life than the soul of man. If, therefore, man’s soul, giving life to every part of the natural body, makes them subject to the head, much more will the spirit of Christ bring the members of his mystical body into subjection to himself. “If the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from the dead is living in you#super[+], He who raised Christ Jesus from the dead #footnote[Rom 8:11.] will also give life to your mortal bodies through His Spirit, who lives in you.”

By this let trial be made of particular visible churches and of particular persons, whether they are indeed of this true universal #footnote[#emph[Use.] Trial of true churches.] church or not. Those visible churches that refuse to be governed by Christ’s word and are wholly governed by human traditions, that rise against Christ and play the adulteress by committing idolatry, are not of this universal church which is #emph[subject to Christ.] No more are infidels who defy Christ, heretics who deny him, ignorant persons who do not know his will, profane persons who despise him, worldlings who think lightly of him, or any who persecute or scorn him in his members. By this we may see that many have a name for being of the church who indeed are not.

#emph[Objection.] Many such persons may belong to God’s election, and so be of that body of which Christ is a Savior.

#emph[Answer.] Election indeed gives them a title to Christ, but they cannot reap any benefit by that title until they have possession of Christ by virtue of their spiritual union with him. Nor can they have any assurance of their election until they find, by the life-giving power of the spirit, that they are united to Christ. Therefore, so long as men remain destitute of the Spirit of Christ and are possessed by a contrary spirit, they may well be judged, for the present, to be none of this body and to have no part in Christ; their future state is left to him who alone knows what it will be.

=== §. 25. #emph[Of the extent of the church’s subjection.]

The extent of the church’s subjection to Christ is without any restriction at all: #emph[in everything.] For there is nothing that Christ requires of her but she may yield to it with a good conscience, and must yield to it in obedience. Just and pure and perfect are all his commandments; there is no error in any of them; no injury or harm can follow from keeping them. This extent being taken for granted here, I may further infer that

#emph[Those who are of the true universal church will yield complete] #footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[obedience to Christ:] they will obey him in each and every one of his commandments. David “had not turned aside from anything #footnote[1 Kgs 15:5.] the #smallcaps[Lord] commanded.” Josiah “turned to the #smallcaps[Lord] with all his #footnote[2 Kgs 23:25. Luke 1:6.] heart … according to all the Law.” And Zechariah and Elizabeth walked “in all the commandments” of God. All these were of this church, and all others who are of this church are of their mind.

For the spirit of Christ which is in them works a thorough #footnote[Reason.] reformation. Even as the flesh leads a natural man on to every sin, so the spirit of Christ stirs him up to every good duty. In this respect it is said that “anyone born of God #footnote[1 John 3:9.] does not commit sin.”

#emph[Objection.] The best saints in all ages have transgressed in many #footnote[Jas 3:2.] things.

#emph[Answer.] Their sins, though grievous, have not been committed willfully, in open rebellion against Christ; rather, they have fallen into them partly through their own weakness, and partly through the violence of some temptation. So what the apostle says of himself may be applied to all who are of the body of Christ: “What I do, I do not approve. … In that case, #footnote[Rom 7:15, 17.] it is no longer I who do it, but it is sin living in me that does it.”

This extent is a good proof of the truth of subjection, for #footnote[#emph[Use.] Trial of integrity.] in this lies a main difference between the upright and the hypocrite, indeed between restraining and renewing grace. The restraining grace that is in many hypocrites stirs them to do #emph[many] things that Christ commands, at least if those things do not cross their honor, profit, ease and the like. Herod, that notorious hypocrite,#footnote[Mark 6:20.] “did many things.” There is none who bears the name of the church but will be subject in some things. But none but the upright, who are indeed renewed by the sanctifying spirit of Christ, will make Christ’s will their rule in all things and hold close to it in everything, preferring it before their pleasure, profit, preferment, or any other outward allurement. Those who do so give good evidence that they are of the body of Christ, and may rest assured that Christ is their #emph[Savior.]
